  "style": {
    "name": "Ember Sun-Shrine",
    "rationale": "A polychrome Mesoamerican-inflected sun temple: warm orange terracotta wall, teal prismarine pilasters with gold capitals, cyan and yellow glazed bands, lapis window heads, sea-lantern glow behind the windows, and a verdigris oxidized-copper crown. Bay rhythm is 1-3-6-3-1 pilaster-framed, mirrored about x=15.5. Relief is built by exclusion: a deepslate core sits at z1-2, the skin at z3, and projecting pilasters, bands, sills, and the lintel at z4-5, so windows and the door recede to z2 with no air placements. The ground plinth is polished blackstone, the entrance is a stepped gold-and-yellow arch with red-nether jambs and a dark-prismarine door recess, and the pediment steps up in 10-wide and 6-wide tiers with copper stairs at the shoulders and a gold finial as the crown. Facade is 32 wide, 24 high, and 5 deep. Every stair and slab carries its facing or half state."
  },
